- Run `git status` to see the in-progress rebase and the conflicted files.
- Resolve each conflict carefully: read the whole file, integrate BOTH sides (keep the
  intent of your change AND the incoming changes from origin). Do NOT blindly pick one
  side or delete code to make the markers (`<<<<<<<`/`=======`/`>>>>>>>`) go away.
- **Exception — duplicate fix:** if the conflict exists because the mainline already
  landed the same fix our branch carries (two independent spellings of one repair),
  integrating both sides produces permanent divergence that re-conflicts on every
  subsequent rebase. Take the mainline's version and drop ours — that is
  `git checkout --ours <file>`, NOT `--theirs`. Git swaps the two during a rebase:
  `--ours` is the branch being rebased ONTO (the mainline), `--theirs` is our own work
  being replayed. Reaching for `--theirs` keeps the side that was meant to be dropped,
  resolves the file silently, and brings the identical conflict back on every later
  rebase. To confirm the right side was taken: after `git add <file>`,
  `git diff --cached HEAD -- <file>` is empty. Record what was dropped and the upstream
  commit that supersedes it in the rebase commit message.
  **Evidence test (mid-rebase):** run `git log --oneline -5 HEAD -- <file>` — `HEAD`
  during a rebase is the mainline side. If an upstream commit in that list applies
  what our change applies, the conflict is a duplicate fix.
  If a `DUPLICATE-WORK CANDIDATES` block is present in the rebase output, it lists
  exactly these files and commits — use it as a shortcut. Its absence does not change
  the rule: the evidence test above is authoritative whether or not the block exists.
  Absent clear evidence that the conflict is a duplicate fix, keep both sides — the
  preceding bullet's rule applies.
- `git add` each resolved file, then `git rebase --continue`. Repeat until the rebase is
  fully applied (it may stop on several commits).
- Then run `{{.VerifyCmd}}` and make it pass on the merged result — a rebase can introduce
  SEMANTIC conflicts git did not flag.
- Leave the worktree clean and the rebase complete. Do NOT `git push` (a later step
  pushes) and do NOT `git rebase --abort` (that throws away the rebase progress).
